1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The Māori saw — a new year.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Since 2022, New Zealand has had a public
holiday called Matariki.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Each April and May, it sinks into the
evening twilight and hides in the Sun’s
glare.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:31,000
In June it returns before sunrise, in
the southern winter. The old year ends;
a new one begins.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Many accounts count seven.

6
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Lately, nine get the most attention:
Māori astronomer Rangi Mātāmua drew them
from a 1933 family manuscript.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Food from the soil. Fruit and birds.
Fresh water. The sea. Rain. Wind.

8
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Matariki, the mother: health.
Hiwa-i-te-rangi: new year wishes.
Pōhutukawa: those lost this past year.

9
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Those last two are the faintest: you
need a truly dark sky.

10
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
The Pleiades sit low in the
east-northeast — as New Zealand greets
its new year.

11
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:27,000
The Milky Way: a river, a road, a rift.

12
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

